course: dictionary
lesson: 15428
title: Making things the same
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Standardised is a long word, but you can break it into parts. Standard means a rule or level that everything is measured against. The ending -ised means made into. So standardised means made to fit one rule, or done the same way for everyone. «All pupils sat the same standardised test in May.» The clue is the same: every pupil answered the same questions.
explain: Ask yourself why something would be standardised. «The results can be compared» is the reason. If every pupil does the same test, the teacher can see who scored more and who scored less. Swap in your meaning: «All pupils sat the same test that was made the same way for everyone» still makes sense, so you have it.
explain: The word can also describe things in history and science. «Long ago, spellings were standardised, so words were written the same way everywhere.» «Scientists use standardised units, such as metres and grams, so their experiments can be repeated anywhere.» In both cases, one rule makes things fit together.
fact: Standardise is a verb, and standardised is its past form: «Dictionaries standardised spelling.»
fact: As an adjective, «a standardised test» is the same for every pupil, so the results can be compared.
fact: Standard means a rule or level used to measure things. The ending -ised means made into.
fact: In England, SATs are standardised tests. Many state tests in the USA are standardised too.
fact: Standardised units let experiments be repeated in any place, because everyone measures in the same way.
exercise: «The test was standardised, so every pupil answered the same questions.» What does standardised mean here? | *Done the same way for everyone | Lost and found | Very long and hard | Made up on the spot
exercise: Why are standardised tests used in schools? | So every pupil gets a different test | *So results can be compared | So pupils can skip the hard questions | So the teacher can finish early
exercise: Mum says her recipe is standardised. What does she mean? | It is only for birthday cakes | It is a very old recipe from long ago | It only has pictures and no measurements | *Everyone who makes it gets the same result
test: «The sizes of the bricks were standardised, so every brick fitted every wall.» What does standardised mean? | Broken into small pieces | Painted in bright colours | *Made the same, so they fit together | Very heavy
test: «Scientists use standardised units, such as metres, so experiments can be repeated.» Which is a standardised unit? | *Metre | Whisper | Happiness | Blue
exam: Which word is the past form of standardise? | *Standardised | Standard | Standardise | Standardising
